  "reason": "The records collectively point toward atomistic mechanical loading of compositionally similar FCC HEAs across temperature and strain-rate regimes: R6 supplies the precedent for very-high-strain-rate MD, R4 concerns elevated-temperature high-rate deformation, and R9 explicitly suggests tensile loading at higher temperatures. R1, R3, and R18 support temperature-dependent softening, while R5 and R12 connect closely related Cantor-type alloys to stacking-fault/FCC–HCP behavior. Most importantly, R20 identifies partial-dislocation motion in CoNiCrFeMn nanowires. However, the set does not specifically disclose the combined bulk single-crystal tensile protocol across crystallographic orientations, temperatures, and rates, nor vacancy-cluster nucleation followed by Shockley partials producing ISFs and ESFs. Thus it points in the right direction but leaves substantial inventive choices and the detailed mechanism unresolved."
